lot of the subplots running through it tie in really well with Werewolf.<br /> Dog Soldiers (2002) - I like all of Neil Marshall's films; in this case, a good example of pack tactics and a reminder of how isolated parts of Scotland are.<br /> An American Werewolf in London (1981) - In addition to being a good werewolf movie in general, it's also a good look at dark animism and hauntings. <br /> The Howling (1981) - A potential idea for a semi-isolated caern, and does tie in with the more sexual aspects of the werewolf legend.<br /> Wolfen (1981) - One of the first films to really tie werewolves in with Native American spiritualism and land issues.<br /> Ginger Snaps (2000) - Interesting characters and examines werewolfism as a metaphor for puberty.<br /> Wolf (1994) - A good inspiration for how different things can be after the first change.<br /> The Wolfman (2010) - A remake of the 1941 classic, with a lot of nice Victorian Gothic atmosphere.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}